  "full_review": "The appealing Norwegian film \"Elling\" (First Look) follows two institutionalized 40-year-old men as they take up independent living in an Oslo apartment.\n\n���� Before the film's title appears we see agoraphobic Elling (Per Christian Ellefsen) cowering in a closet after the death of his mother as the authorities haul him off to a state institution. Two years later, Elling and his beefy, dimwitted roommate, Kjell (Sven Nordin), are deemed fit to survive in the outside world under the occasional eye of a social worker (Jorgen Langhelle) who sets them up in an apartment building, much to Elling's terror. The two immediately try to re-create the institution, ignoring their separate bedrooms and moving both twin beds into one room exactly as they had lived previously.\n\n���� Unwilling to go out for basic grocery shopping or even to answer the phone, Ellingrelies heavily on the coarse but kindly Kjell, who is desperate to experience sex. After Kjell comes to the aid of a pregnant, unmarried neighbor (Marit Pia Jacobsen) and is lovestruck, jealous Elling reluctantly decides to venture out to a poetry reading. Encouraged by a writer (Per Christensen) there who takes no notice of Elling's nervous ways, he starts writing poetry, stuffing his unsigned scribblings into boxes of sauerkraut in the local supermarket.\n\n���� When his writer pal falls ill and expects Elling to help him, the horrified neurotic brings along Kjell for support. Despite their fears and frustrations, Ellingdetermines to nurse his friend and write more poetry while Kjelltakes on repairing the man's vintage car -- and shyly courting the expectant mother.\n\n���� Without being unduly sentimental, director Petter Naess creates a compassionate portrait of two men struggling mightily to cope with the frightening reality of independent living. But the story is conveyed with enormous humor and both Ellefsenand Nordin (who originated the characters on stage) excel in their characterizations. Kjell uses a kind of tough-love approach to calm Elling down when he gets hysterical and Elling unselfishly intervenes to advance Kjell's romance. This aspect, Kjell's determination to lose his virginity, is dealt with in earthy fashion as Elling hears unmistakable sounds of his friend's passion.\n\n���� Although the movie does not suggest they overcome their fears, they do learn to cope, helped by the woman and the writer who accept them as they are. It's also interesting that both men find a positive outlet in creativity: Ellingwith his poetry and Kjell in his surprising ability to make art of out matchsticks.\n\n���� The film points to how even those deemed as misfits have talents and potential and how the patience and kindness of others can make all the difference.\n\n���� The movie's sweet-natured tone overcomes its sometimes salty content and it is heartening to watch these good and decent characters pull together.\n\n���� Subtitles.\n\n���� Because of some casual rough language, an implied sexual encounter, fleeting nudity and crass expressions, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-III -- adults. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is R -- restricted."
